  <h2>The architectural split</h2>
  <p><a href="https://microsoft.github.io/autogen/">Autogen</a> is a Python framework for building multi-agent conversations. You import it, instantiate two or more <code>AssistantAgent</code>s with different system prompts, and run them inside one program with one process and one shared address space. The "agents" are role-prompted invocations of the same underlying LLM client, executed in turns by a coordinator. Everything lives in your Python process.</p>

  <p>agentalk takes the opposite approach. There is no framework you import. Each agent is a separate Claude Code session — possibly on a separate laptop or VM — with its own filesystem, its own running shell, its own MCP servers, its own running services. The connection between them is an HTTPS channel; the bridge stores ciphertext and never reads message content.</p>

  <p>These are not competing implementations of the same idea. They parallelize different things. Autogen parallelizes <em>role specialization</em> inside one program. agentalk parallelizes <em>execution environment</em>.</p>

